# steps — the great south steps: ten risers, cheek walls, raking copings, pedestals at the foot.
#
# The flight everything approaches the building by. x -9.45..9.45 (21 M either side of the centre
# line), z -16.25 to -13.05, ground y = 0.00 to the podium top y = 1.80. That z pair is 0.55 m
# south of the contract's plan table and it is deliberate; the paragraph headed THE CONTRACT
# CONFLICT below is why, and it is the one thing to read before moving anything in this file.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE PUTS UNDER LOAD — the WALKABILITY CHECK, and it is not a decoration on the side.
#
# `measure` looks down every column of the finished clip for the top of the matter, compares
# neighbouring columns, and reports the WORST RISE in the building. These steps are what that
# number is supposed to be: ten risers of EXACTLY 0.18, ten runs of EXACTLY 0.32, treads dead
# level and dead identical, and nothing in this fragment allowed to beat them.
#
# "Nothing allowed to beat them" is a real constraint and it decided four things here. Read this
# before you add anything to this file, because it is very easy to break by accident:
#
#   main.cpp asks for max_step = per_metre * 20 / 100 voxels and counts a rise only when it is
#   <= max_step * 4 — anything taller is treated as a wall rather than as a bad step. At the
#   contract's metre 32 that cutoff is 24 voxels = 0.75 m. So ANY horizontal neighbour pair in
#   this part whose tops differ by between 0.1875 m and 0.75 m sets the building's worst rise
#   instead of the stair. Everything here is therefore either flush, one riser apart, or more
#   than 0.75 m apart. In particular:
#
#   1. THE COPING STANDS 1.26 m ABOVE THE NOSING LINE, not the 0.90 it would like to be. The
#      tread beside it is up to one riser above that line, so 0.90 would have put a 0.72 m rise
#      down the whole length of both cheek walls and that, not the stair, would have been the
#      number. 1.26 - 0.18 = 1.08, clear of the cutoff with 0.33 to spare.
#
#   2. THE PEDESTAL CAP IS LEVEL WITH THE COPING WHERE THEY MEET, both at y = 1.80. A pedestal
#      that stood the usual 0.30-0.40 above the coping it terminates would have put a 12-voxel
#      rise at the junction. Level is also why the pedestal is 0.96 deep in z (three runs) rather
#      than 0.90 square: three runs puts the nosing line at 0.54 there, and 0.54 + 1.26 = 1.80,
#      the podium's own datum, exactly.
#
#   3. THERE IS NO URN ON THE PEDESTALS, and that is a decision, not an omission. The pedestals
#      are sized for the order's `urn` (0.5625 across on a 0.90 cap) and it is one line to add
#      one — but any ornament whose widest point overhangs its own foot puts a rise equal to that
#      overhang beside it. The order's urn is widest 0.585 above its base, so standing one here
#      would have reported a 0.59 m worst rise for the whole facility and buried the stair. The
#      site fragment already stands the order's bronze urns on its gate piers 0.45 m outboard of
#      these pedestals, which is where they belong and where they cost nothing.
#
#   4. THE WALL UNDER THE COPING OVERLAPS IT BY 0.09 AND USED TO ONLY TOUCH IT. Two shapes that
#      meet on a plane are not joined — BRIEF.md rule 4 — and this pair proved it at metre 16 by
#      reporting 3 components and 28 floating voxels, the north tip of each coping adrift. It is
#      not a walkability fault but it is the same family: a join nobody measured.
#
# Secondary loads this part carries, all of them free riders on geometry that had to exist:
#
#   THE JOINTS. 0.0375 m wide (M/12) and 0.045 m deep (M/10) — one and a fifth voxels across at
#   metre 32 — cut on the same 0.675 grid as the podium's terrace paving, so the stair and the
#   floor it arrives at are jointed as one piece of masonry. They are cut by intersecting the
#   joint slabs with a 0.045 SHELL of the flight (`offset by=0.045`, then differenced), so one
#   construction follows every tread, every riser and every nosing round without ten separate
#   cutters. A groove is the hardest thin matter there is: a sampler stepping by its own reported
#   distance can walk straight across the mouth of one. DO NOT WIDEN THEM.
#
#   THEY DID NOT EXIST UNTIL NOW AND NOTHING SAID SO. The sign on that `offset` was wrong — see
#   the long note beside it — so the shell was empty, the joints were empty, and `steps_mass` and
#   `steps_flight` measured the same 2,423,674 voxels at metre 32. A cut that removes nothing is
#   the failure with no symptom: the report is clean, the component count is 1, the materials are
#   right, and the picture is a smooth ramp of stone where the file says masonry.
#
#   THE NOSINGS AND THE WEAR, added with the joints and for the same reason. A tread cut as a
#   plain box has no nosing, and a flight of them is one folded surface with no line of shadow on
#   it. Each tread now oversails its riser by 0.045 (M/10) on a half-round, and each stone of each
#   tread is dished 0.045 in the middle where feet fall, so the joints stand proud the way they do
#   on a stair that has been used. Both are M/10 — one and a half voxels at metre 32 — and both
#   are inset far enough from the nosing line that the walkability metric still sees one riser.
#
#   THE RUSTICATION. Both faces of both cheek walls are channelled to the podium's own numbers —
#   0.045 wide (M/10) and 0.090 deep (M/5), courses of 0.45, blocks of 1.35 in running bond — and
#   on the podium's own course lines, so the two read as one wall. One and a half voxels across at
#   metre 32, and a channel is worse than a fin: it is a surface that folds back on itself inside
#   one sample step, so a sampler that steps by its own distance can cross the mouth of one without
#   ever seeing it. Below metre 24 they do not show at all, and that is the resolution, not a
#   fault. DO NOT WIDEN THEM EITHER.
#
#   THE ROTATED FRAME. The raking coping and the wall under it are one straight section built
#   about the origin and turned by -0.08154932 turns about x — atan(0.18/0.32), the stair's own
#   slope. A rotation is an isometry so the field stays exact and keeps a box; this is the only
#   rotated mass in the lower half of the building and it is what will say so if rotated bounds
#   or box settling regress.
#
#   THE MITRES. The pedestal's two moulding bands are each the INTERSECTION of the same section
#   run along z and run along x, so they turn all four corners as a true mitre rather than as the
#   fat square corner four straight runs give. Same trick the podium uses on its cornice, at a
#   twentieth of the size.
#
#   MATERIALS. Granite where it is walked and weathered, polished marble on the copings and caps,
#   and porphyry in the sunk panels of the pedestal dies — a rough stone, a smooth stone and a
#   saturated coloured stone within half a metre of each other in full sun, which is a fairer
#   comparison for a path tracer than any of them alone.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE MODULE. D = 0.90, M = 0.45. Every number below is a multiple or a simple fraction of one of
# those, or of the stair's own two contract numbers, the riser 0.18 (0.4 M) and the run 0.32.
# The only exceptions are constructions rather than dimensions, and each is named where it occurs:
# the rake angle 0.08154932 turns (= atan(0.5625) / tau), the +-3.60 and -2.97 lengths of the
# section that is turned and then trimmed, and the 8.10 / 9.90 / -0.45 / -0.50 / 4.05 clipping
# frames, which are only knives and are clear of everything they cut by at least 0.45.
#
# WHERE THIS PART MEETS ITS NEIGHBOURS
#
#   the ground (site)   y = 0.00. Everything here is founded at y = -0.18, one riser into it.
#   the podium          y = 1.80 terrace, south face z = -13.05. The top nosing lands ON that
#                       face and the top tread runs north to z = -12.96, which is 0.09 (M/5)
#                       inside the podium, so the joint cannot open.
#   the site            gravel forecourt, boundary balustrade at z -16.20..-15.75 and gate piers
#                       at x 10.35..10.80. The flight is x +-9.45, so the whole 0.90 m way
#                       through beside each cheek is clear and nothing here reaches a pier. The
#                       bottom NOSING reaches -16.295, 0.095 south of where site.clip's gravel
#                       coat stops; that strip of ground reads lawn and is asked for in
#                       requests/steps.md, because a coat in somebody else's file is theirs.
#
# THE CONTRACT CONFLICT IS SETTLED AND THIS FILE IS THE HALF THAT MOVED. It used to run
# z -15.70..-12.50, the contract's numbers, while podium.clip put the podium's south face at
# z = -13.05 — 0.55 m further south — and site.clip laid its gravel, its basins, its hedges and
# its return plinths to that same -13.05. Two fragments of three were built to the podium's line
# and one to the contract's, and the one was this one. The cost, measured before the move:
#
#   the podium's corona occupies y 1.50..1.80 for all z >= -13.05, so it swallowed tread ten
#   whole and all but 0.09 m of tread nine; the flight read as eight treads of 0.32, one tread
#   of 0.09, and then the terrace.
#
# So every z in this file is 0.55 m further south than it was: ten risers of 0.18 and ten runs of
# 0.32 is 3.20 m, the flight runs z = -16.25 at the ground to z = -13.05 at the top nosing, and
# the top tread IS the podium's own face. Nothing else about the part MOVED — the risers, the
# runs, the section of the cheek and the pedestal are all what they were, only 0.55 further on.
# `_contract.clip` still says -15.70..-12.50 in its plan table and should be corrected; that file
# belongs to nobody in particular and is asked for in clips/facility/requests/steps.md.
#
# There is no `void_steps`. This part removes nothing from anybody, and the manifest's `hollowed`
# difference does not ask for one. Every `difference` below takes matter out of one of this file's
# own shapes and out of nothing else.

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE FLIGHT — ten risers of 0.18, ten runs of 0.32, y -0.18 to 1.80
# ================================================================================================
#
# Ten nested boxes, each set back one run and raised one riser from the one below it, each running
# north to z = -12.96 so that it is buried by everything above it and the last one by the podium.
# Written out rather than swept, because the whole point of this part is that the ten treads are
# IDENTICAL and the ten risers are IDENTICAL, and a box is the only thing in the language that
# cannot drift.
#
#   `stairs` would have been one line, and it is not used on purpose. sd_stairs returns
#   max(box, y - top_of_the_tread_you_are_over), which for a point just south of a riser and well
#   above the tread below reports the height above that tread — a gross OVER-statement of the
#   distance to the riser face a centimetre away. Over-stating is the dangerous direction: it says
#   "nothing near here" where there is something near here. See requests/steps.md.
#
#   tread  1   z -16.25   top 0.18        tread  6   z -14.65   top 1.08
#   tread  2   z -15.93   top 0.36        tread  7   z -14.33   top 1.26
#   tread  3   z -15.61   top 0.54        tread  8   z -14.01   top 1.44
#   tread  4   z -15.29   top 0.72        tread  9   z -13.69   top 1.62
#   tread  5   z -14.97   top 0.90        tread 10   z -13.37   top 1.80
#
# MEASURED at metre 32, on the centre line, off a 1:1 section — not read off the list above. On
# `--part steps_treads`, the ten boxes with nothing added and nothing cut out of them:
#
#   ten goings   eight quantise to 0.3125 and two to 0.34375   mean 0.319, against 0.32 drawn
#   nine risers  seven quantise to 0.1875 and two to 0.15625   mean 0.181, against 0.18 drawn
#
# Six voxels is what a 0.18 riser can be at metre 32 — 0.18 x 32 = 5.76 — and no placement of the
# treads makes nine consecutive ones land on five every time.
#
# And on `--part steps_flight`, the finished thing with the joints, the nosings and the wear cut
# into it, the largest step between neighbouring sample columns anywhere down the centre line is
# 0.1875. One riser. Nothing this file adds beats the stair, which is the whole constraint above.
#
# The player is 1.80 tall, 0.60 across and climbs 0.55. This is a third of that.

# THE TREADS RUN OUT TO 9.3375, NOT TO 9.45. That is the outer face of the cheek wall, and the
# wall stands from the ground rather than on the steps, so the whole of each tread's end is buried
# inside it and the flank of the flight is the wall's own raking face. Built the other way round —
# full-width treads with a narrower wall sitting on them — every tread left a 0.1125 ledge sticking
# out round the pedestal at the foot, three of them stacked like a staircase nobody asked for. It
# is obvious on a contact sheet and invisible in every number.

let steps_tread_01 = box -9.3375 -0.18 -16.25   9.3375 0.18 -12.96 round=0.015
let steps_tread_02 = box -9.3375 -0.18 -15.93   9.3375 0.36 -12.96 round=0.015
let steps_tread_03 = box -9.3375 -0.18 -15.61   9.3375 0.54 -12.96 round=0.015
let steps_tread_04 = box -9.3375 -0.18 -15.29   9.3375 0.72 -12.96 round=0.015
let steps_tread_05 = box -9.3375 -0.18 -14.97   9.3375 0.90 -12.96 round=0.015
let steps_tread_06 = box -9.3375 -0.18 -14.65   9.3375 1.08 -12.96 round=0.015
let steps_tread_07 = box -9.3375 -0.18 -14.33   9.3375 1.26 -12.96 round=0.015
let steps_tread_08 = box -9.3375 -0.18 -14.01   9.3375 1.44 -12.96 round=0.015
let steps_tread_09 = box -9.3375 -0.18 -13.69   9.3375 1.62 -12.96 round=0.015
let steps_tread_10 = box -9.3375 -0.18 -13.37   9.3375 1.80 -12.96 round=0.015

let steps_treads = union {
    steps_tread_01 steps_tread_02 steps_tread_03 steps_tread_04 steps_tread_05
    steps_tread_06 steps_tread_07 steps_tread_08 steps_tread_09 steps_tread_10
}

# The arris round is 0.015 (M/30) and it is chosen against the voxel grid, not by eye. At metre 32
# the first sample column north of a nosing sits between 0.006 and 0.028 from it, so the deepest
# the round pulls a tread top down at a sample is 3.3 mm — a tenth of a voxel, and never enough to
# move a tread into the voxel below. A 0.03 round would have cost a voxel on four of the ten
# treads and made the reported rise 7 voxels instead of 6.

# --- the nosings -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A tread cut as a plain box has no nosing at all: its front arris is the top of the riser below
# it, and the whole flight is one folded surface with a 15 mm break on it. A stone step is not
# that. The tread STONE oversails the riser and its front is worked to a half-round, so there is a
# line of shadow under every nosing the length of the flight, and that line is most of what tells
# a person at thirty metres that there are ten steps rather than a ramp.
#
# 0.045 out (M/10) and 0.045 deep, rounded 0.018 — the same M/10 as the rustication channel and
# the joints, and for the same reason: at metre 32 it is one and a half voxels, which is the size
# this building exists to sample. NOT 0.03, which is the projection a nosing this size would take
# in stone: 0.03 x 32 is 0.96 of a voxel, and a lip that is under one voxel anywhere along its
# length is a lip that dithers rather than one that reads.
#
# IT DOES NOT MOVE THE STAIR. The walkable top of tread k now runs from z_k - 0.045 to
# z_(k+1) - 0.045, which is the same 0.32; the rise from tread to tread is the same 0.18; and the
# undercut is 0.045 of overhang, far under the 0.1875 m the walkability metric can see.

let steps_nose_01 = box -9.3375 0.135 -16.295   9.3375 0.18 -16.25 round=0.018
let steps_nose_02 = box -9.3375 0.315 -15.975   9.3375 0.36 -15.93 round=0.018
let steps_nose_03 = box -9.3375 0.495 -15.655   9.3375 0.54 -15.61 round=0.018
let steps_nose_04 = box -9.3375 0.675 -15.335   9.3375 0.72 -15.29 round=0.018
let steps_nose_05 = box -9.3375 0.855 -15.015   9.3375 0.90 -14.97 round=0.018
let steps_nose_06 = box -9.3375 1.035 -14.695   9.3375 1.08 -14.65 round=0.018
let steps_nose_07 = box -9.3375 1.215 -14.375   9.3375 1.26 -14.33 round=0.018
let steps_nose_08 = box -9.3375 1.395 -14.055   9.3375 1.44 -14.01 round=0.018
let steps_nose_09 = box -9.3375 1.575 -13.735   9.3375 1.62 -13.69 round=0.018
let steps_nose_10 = box -9.3375 1.755 -13.415   9.3375 1.80 -13.37 round=0.018

let steps_nosings = union {
    steps_nose_01 steps_nose_02 steps_nose_03 steps_nose_04 steps_nose_05
    steps_nose_06 steps_nose_07 steps_nose_08 steps_nose_09 steps_nose_10
}

# The flight in the rough: the treads and their nosings, before anything is cut out of them. This
# is the control arm every cut below is measured against — see requests/steps-cuts-probe.clip.
let steps_mass = union { steps_treads steps_nosings }

# --- the joints ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 0.0375 wide, 0.045 deep, on odd multiples of 0.675 — the podium's own paving grid, so a joint in
# the top tread runs straight on into a joint in the terrace. Thirteen blocks of 1.35 across the
# clear width, with the outermost joint at 8.775 falling under the cheek wall where it is never
# seen; that is what makes the visible field of treads symmetric about a block rather than about a
# joint.
#
# Cut against a shell of the flight rather than against the flight itself, so that one slab per
# joint follows tread, nosing and riser alike and stops 0.045 in. A joint slab cut straight through
# the mass would have sawn the stair into fourteen pieces.
#
# `by=0.045` AND NOT `by=-0.045`, AND UNTIL NOW IT WAS THE WRONG ONE. `Op::Offset` returns
# `eval(child) + by` (src/forge/field.cpp:1314), so a POSITIVE `by` adds to the distance and pulls
# the surface INWARD. `offset { steps_mass } by=-0.045` therefore grew the flight by 45 mm, the
# "core" came out bigger than the mass it was carved from, `difference { mass core }` was
# consequently EMPTY, and so was every joint. Measured at metre 32: `steps_mass` 2,423,674 voxels,
# `steps_flight` 2,423,674 voxels — the cut removed nothing at all, and nothing said so. No error,
# no floating component, no material missing from the report; the joints simply were not there and
# the file's own header said they were.
#
# `clips/facility/BRIEF.md` line 227 still documents this as *"offset { a } by=-0.05  # shrinks or
# grows without rounding"*, which is what put the sign here; requests/dome.md measured the truth
# and asked for the wording to be changed to *POSITIVE SHRINKS*. Until that lands, check the sign
# of every `offset` by measuring the volume of the thing it makes.
#
# The joints exist now and the simplest proof of it is two sections. `--slice x@4.05`, down the
# middle of a stone, reads tread tops 1.8125, 1.625, 1.4375, 1.25; `--slice x@3.375`, down a
# joint, reads 1.75, 1.5625, 1.4062, 1.2188 — one voxel lower, the whole way up the flight, with
# the same 0.1875 between treads. That is a groove, cut to depth, following every tread and every
# riser, and it was not there this morning.

let steps_shell_core = offset { steps_mass } by=0.045
let steps_shell      = difference { steps_mass steps_shell_core }

let steps_joint_slab = box -0.01875 -1.00 -20.00   0.01875 3.00 20.00
let steps_joint_grid = mirror { translate { repeat { steps_joint_slab } x=1.35 nx=7 } 0.675 0 0 } axis=x
let steps_joints     = intersection { steps_joint_grid steps_shell }

# --- the wear -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A stone stair that has been walked on for two hundred years is dished. Not smoothly and not
# everywhere: each tread is laid as separate stones, each stone wears in its own middle, and the
# joints between them stand slightly proud because nobody's foot lands on a joint. That is the
# thing that most makes worked stone read as scanned rather than modelled, and it is free here
# because the stones are already laid out — the wear grid is the JOINT grid, offset half a block.
#
# One hollow per stone per tread: 1.08 (2.4 M) across of a 1.35 block, so 0.135 of unworn margin
# either side of it meets the joint; 0.1575 (0.35 M) of the 0.32 run, starting 0.045 (M/10) behind
# the nosing; 0.045 (M/10) deep, coved 0.045 all round so the floor of it tapers up to the tread
# rather than ending in a step.
#
# 0.045 IS UNDER THE WALKABILITY METRIC AND THE INSETS ARE WHY. `walkability()` reports the worst
# rise between neighbouring columns that is <= max_step * 4 (0.75 m at metre 32), so ANY rise
# between 0.1875 and 0.75 anywhere in the building becomes the number instead of the stair. A
# hollow 0.045 deep is a quarter of that on its own — but a hollow that reaches the NEXT tread's
# nosing puts 0.18 + 0.045 = 0.225 beside it, which is squarely in the window.
#
# IT DID. The first cut of this ran 0.045 to 0.27 of the run, which looked like 0.05 of clearance
# from the riser at 0.32 — and it is not, because the tread above oversails its riser by 0.045, so
# the real clearance was 0.005 m. The section at metre 32 came back with SEVEN-voxel rises
# (0.2188) at five of the nine junctions, up from six. 0.2025 (0.45 M) leaves 0.0725 — better than
# two voxels of untouched tread in front of every nosing — and the flight measures 0.1875 again.
# Anything added to a tread has to answer this arithmetic: the walkable surface of tread k runs
# from z_k - 0.045 to z_k + 0.275, and both ends of it belong to the nosings.
#
# nx=6, so the hollows run out to a stone centred on 8.10 and the last strip of tread — 8.775 out
# to the cheek wall — is left plain. It is under the wall and never seen.

let steps_dish     = box -0.54 -0.045 0.045   0.54 0.09 0.2025 round=0.045
let steps_dish_row = repeat { steps_dish } x=1.35 nx=6

let steps_wear = union {
    translate { steps_dish_row } 0 0.18 -16.25
    translate { steps_dish_row } 0 0.36 -15.93
    translate { steps_dish_row } 0 0.54 -15.61
    translate { steps_dish_row } 0 0.72 -15.29
    translate { steps_dish_row } 0 0.90 -14.97
    translate { steps_dish_row } 0 1.08 -14.65
    translate { steps_dish_row } 0 1.26 -14.33
    translate { steps_dish_row } 0 1.44 -14.01
    translate { steps_dish_row } 0 1.62 -13.69
    translate { steps_dish_row } 0 1.80 -13.37
}

let steps_flight = difference { steps_mass steps_joints steps_wear }

# TWO CUTTERS IN ONE `difference`, AND HERE IS WHAT EACH OF THEM TAKES. Measured at metre 32
# through clips/facility/requests/steps-cuts-probe.clip, which carries a binding that omits one
# cut at a time so the arms can be subtracted:
#
#   steps_mass                                    2,426,648 voxels
#   less the joints only  (probe_joints_only)     2,417,948      -8,700   0.2657 m3
#   less the wear only    (probe_wear_only)       2,394,666     -31,982   0.9766 m3
#   less both             (steps_flight)          2,385,966     -40,682 = the sum, to the voxel
#
# Additive means disjoint: the wear does not eat a joint and the joints do not deepen the wear.
# One component in every arm, none floating.
#
# The joint cut is small in volume and that is the point of it — fourteen grooves one and a fifth
# voxels wide, each following the whole folded length of the flight, 0.27 cubic metres out of 74.
# What it buys is surface, which is the same trade the podium's grooves make. On the whole part at
# metre 32: 234.79 m2 of surface before this change and 280.22 m2 after, for 79.73 m3 of stone
# before and 78.97 after. Nineteen per cent more surface for one per cent less matter. If those
# two figures ever converge, the grooves have started dissolving in the sampler.

# ================================================================================================
# THE CHEEK — built once on the east side about x = 9.00, folded at the end
# ================================================================================================
#
# Across the cheek, from the centre line at x = 9.00:
#
#   0.4500 (M)      the coping and the pedestal's plinth and cap — outer face at x = 9.45, which
#                   is the contract's own edge of the steps
#   0.3375 (0.75 M) the wall and the pedestal's die, one continuous 0.675 slab from the ground to
#                   the coping, oversailed 0.1125 (M/4) on BOTH faces by the coping above it
#
# so a flank reads: a moulded pedestal at the foot, a plain raking wall out of it, and a coping
# with a shadow under both its edges. The treads die into the wall from inside; the wall is the
# flank, and there is no stepped edge on the outside of the flight at all.

# --- the rake -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The coping's top plane is the line of the nosings raised 1.26 (2.8 M):
#
#     y = 0.5625 * (z + 16.25) + 1.26        0.5625 = 0.18 / 0.32, the stair's own slope
#
# which is y = 1.80 at z = -15.29 (the pedestal's north face, three runs up) and y = 3.06 at
# z = -13.05 (the podium face, where it stands 1.26 above the terrace).
#
# Built as a straight section along z about the origin and turned about x by the rake angle. The
# ANGLE IS NEGATIVE: `rotate` moves the shape, so a positive turn about x carries local +z toward
# -y, and this coping has to climb. 0.08154932 turns is atan(0.5625) / tau; it is a construction,
# not a dimension, in the same way the order's entasis radius is.
#
# Local y = 0 is the top of the coping and local z runs along the rake. The origin of the turned
# section is carried to (9.00, 3.06, -13.05), a point on the top plane, so every other number in
# the section is measured from a face rather than from an arbitrary datum.
#
#   local  0.00 .. -0.18   the coping proper, 0.45 out (M)
#   local -0.18 .. -0.27   a cavetto splay back to the wall face, 0.09 (M/5) of it
#   local -0.18 .. -2.97   the wall, 0.3375 out, carried far enough down to be trimmed flat
#
# THE WALL'S TOP IS AT LOCAL -0.18 AND NOT AT -0.27, WHICH IS WHERE THE COPING'S UNDERSIDE IS.
# It used to be -0.27 — the two met on a plane and touched without overlapping, which BRIEF.md
# rule 4 forbids in as many words. It does not fail loudly: at metre 32 the pair still voxelizes
# as one solid. At metre 16 it does not, and `--part part_steps` reported **3 components, 28
# floating voxels** — fourteen at each of (+-9.44, 3.06, -12.44), the north tip of each coping,
# cut adrift from the wall it sits on by a joint that was exactly zero thick. Raising the wall to
# -0.18 buries it 0.09 (M/5) inside the coping's own core, which occupies the identical 0.675
# width, so nothing about the finished section changes and the two are one piece at any metre.

let steps_cope_band  = box -0.45 -0.18 -3.60   0.45 0.00 3.60 round=0.015
let steps_cope_core  = box -0.3375 -0.27 -3.60   0.3375 -0.18 3.60
let steps_cope_splay = mirror { cavetto 0.3375 -0.18 -3.60   0.45 -0.27 3.60 run=z } axis=x
let steps_cope_sec   = union { steps_cope_band steps_cope_core steps_cope_splay }
let steps_wall_sec   = box -0.3375 -2.97 -3.60   0.3375 -0.18 3.60 round=0.012

let steps_cope_laid = translate { rotate { steps_cope_sec } x=-0.08154932 } 9.00 3.06 -13.05
let steps_wall_laid = translate { rotate { steps_wall_sec } x=-0.08154932 } 9.00 3.06 -13.05

# The two are trimmed to different lengths on purpose. The coping stops dead at the pedestal's
# north face and dies into it; the wall runs 0.48 further south, into the middle of the pedestal,
# where it is the same 0.675 across as the die and merges with it invisibly. Trim the wall at the
# pedestal face instead and a 0.1125 slot opens between the two.
#
# The frames themselves are only a knife: -0.45 to 4.05 in y and 8.10 to 9.90 in x are clear of
# everything they cut by at least 0.45.
#
# THEIR NORTH FACE IS AT -12.875 AND IT IS THERE BECAUSE OF THE VOXEL GRID, not because of the
# module. -12.875 is 0.175 inside the podium's face, so the coping and the wall end buried in that
# slab rather than stopped against it — and 12.875 is a whole number of voxels at metre 8, 16 and
# 32 alike (it is 103/8), which the treads' own -12.96 is not.
#
# That matters here and nowhere else in the file, because this is the only RAKING top in the part.
# The sampler decides a cell by its centre with a thin-feature rescue, not by coverage, so a trim
# plane that lands mid-cell leaves one partial slice in which only the rescued voxels survive. On
# a raking coping the surviving voxel is the single one at the top of the rake, and it is
# DIAGONALLY adjacent to the run in the slice behind it — which is not adjacency. Measured at
# metre 32 with the trim at -12.96: `steps_cope_solid` came back as 2 components, 28 voxels
# floating at (8.56, 3.09, -12.97), one row of them along the whole width of the coping's tip. The
# wall under it had the same fault, 22 voxels. Neither is visible in the building — both are
# inside the podium — and both are exactly the kind of thing the component count exists to catch.
let steps_cope_zone = box 8.10 -0.45 -15.29   9.90 4.05 -12.875
let steps_wall_zone = box 8.10 -0.18 -15.77   9.90 4.05 -12.875

let steps_cope_solid = intersection { steps_cope_laid steps_cope_zone }
let steps_wall_cut   = intersection { steps_wall_laid steps_wall_zone }

# --- the coping's own joints ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 2.41 m of raking coping in one piece is an extruded plastic handrail, not four stones. So the
# same groove the treads get — 0.0375 wide (M/12), 0.045 deep (M/10) — is cut across it every two
# runs, 0.64 m, on planes of constant z. A mason cuts a raking coping's joints square to the bed
# and not square to the rake, and a vertical plane is what that is.
#
# Cut against a 0.045 SHELL of the coping and not through it, for the same reason as the treads:
# a slab straight through would leave four loose stones and a component count of eight. The sign
# on `by` is the one the treads' note is about — POSITIVE SHRINKS.
#
# The phase is set from the top: joints at -13.37 + 0.64k, which puts one on the top tread's own
# nosing line and reads as four stones of 0.64, 0.64, 0.64 and 0.50. Measured at metre 32, the
# coping is 20,178 voxels before the joints and 19,716 after — 462 voxels, 0.0141 m3, one
# component either way.
let steps_cope_shell  = difference { steps_cope_solid offset { steps_cope_solid } by=0.045 }
let steps_cope_jslab  = box 8.00 -1.00 -0.01875   10.00 5.00 0.01875
let steps_cope_joints = intersection { translate { repeat { steps_cope_jslab } z=0.64 nz=3 }
                                       0 0 -13.37
                                       steps_cope_shell }

let steps_coping_e = difference { steps_cope_solid steps_cope_joints }

# RUSTICATION. The outer face of a cheek wall is 2.30 m long and up to 2.80 m tall with nothing on
# it, which at that size reads as a poured retaining wall rather than as masonry — and it stands
# directly in front of the podium's rusticated die, which does not. So both faces of both cheeks
# are channelled in the podium's own idiom and to the podium's own numbers:
#
#   channel   0.045 wide (M/10), 0.090 deep (M/5)   — the podium's rustication joint exactly
#   course    0.45 (M) tall, blocks 1.35 (3 M) long — three to one, the podium's block
#   bond      running: every other course is moved half a block along
#
# THE COURSE LINES LAND ON THE PODIUM'S. Its die is channelled at y = 0.45, 0.90 and 1.35; mine
# continue the same set up to 2.70, so the coursing of a cheek wall and of the podium behind it are
# one wall seen twice, not two walls that nearly agree.
#
# LEVEL courses, not raking ones. A raking parapet is built of level beds with the coping raking
# over them; courses drawn parallel to the coping read as a ramp of stone rather than as a wall,
# and that is the single thing that most makes a stair look extruded.
#
# At metre 32 the channel is one and a half voxels across and just under three deep, which is the
# hardest kind of thin matter there is — a surface that folds back on itself inside one sample
# step. DO NOT WIDEN IT. Below metre 24 it will not show at all, and that is the sampler's
# resolution rather than a fault in the wall.
#
# The whole thing stops at z = -15.29 so it never reaches the pedestal, which is a dressed block
# and has no courses; 0.09 into a 0.675 wall leaves 0.495 of it, so nothing is cut through.
#
# AND IT CANNOT REACH ANYTHING BUT THE WALL, whatever the zone says. `steps_rust_zone` runs north
# to -12.55, which is half a metre INSIDE the podium — but this shape is only ever the second
# operand of `difference { steps_wall_cut steps_rustic }`, so the most it can do is fail to cut
# the wall. Measured: the cheek wall is 135,936 voxels before the channels and 130,618 after, and
# the podium is the same 6,369,428 voxels either way.

let steps_chan_h  = box 0.2475 -0.0225 -4.00    0.50 0.0225 4.00
let steps_chan_v  = box 0.2475  0.0000 -0.0225  0.50 0.4500 0.0225

let steps_beds    = repeat { steps_chan_h } y=0.45 ny=8
let steps_perps_a = repeat { repeat { steps_chan_v } z=1.35 nz=3 } y=0.90 ny=4
let steps_perps_b = translate { steps_perps_a } 0 0.45 0.675

let steps_rust_sec  = union { steps_beds steps_perps_a steps_perps_b }
let steps_rust_zone = box 8.10 -0.50 -15.29   9.90 4.05 -12.55
let steps_rustic    = intersection { translate { mirror { steps_rust_sec } axis=x } 9.00 0.45 -13.15
                                     steps_rust_zone }

let steps_wall_e = difference { steps_wall_cut steps_rustic }

# --- the pedestal -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 0.90 (D) across and 0.96 (three runs) deep, top at 1.80 — the podium's own datum, and the height
# the raking coping arrives at, so the two meet flush and the walkability check sees no rise there
# at all. The bottom three treads run into it from behind and are entirely inside it.
# Drawn about its own centre so that the mouldings can be mitred, then carried to (9.00, -15.77).
#
#   -0.180 .. 0.2250   plinth        M/2 of it above ground, one riser below
#    0.225 .. 0.3375   cavetto       M/4, plinth face in to the die face
#    0.3375.. 1.3500   die           2.25 M, with a sunk panel on three faces
#    1.350 .. 1.4850   ovolo         0.3 M, the bed mould, die face out to the cap
#    1.485 .. 1.8000   cap           0.7 M, flush with the plinth at 0.45 / 0.48
#
# 0.225 + 0.1125 + 1.0125 + 0.135 + 0.315 = 1.80 exactly.
#
# THE MITRE. Each moulding band is the intersection of the same section run along z and run along
# x, each unioned with a core column so that the band includes the middle rather than only its own
# rim. Intersect two bands that each say "|across| <= f(height)" and what is left is a rectangle
# that grows with height — which is exactly what a moulding returning round four corners is.
#
# THE CORE COLUMNS ARE NOT OPTIONAL AND THEY FAIL SILENTLY. A moulding is only the band between its
# two corners; on its own it says "0.3375 <= |x| <= f(y)", with a hole up the middle. Intersect two
# of those and all you get is the four corners. Drop one core — I had `steps_bed_cz` written with
# the same number twice, so it was a box of zero height and therefore nothing — and the mitre
# quietly becomes two bars on two faces with the other two faces left as a plain overhang. It costs
# no components, no floating voxels and no error, and it is only visible on a contact sheet.

let steps_ped_plinth = box -0.45  -0.18 -0.48    0.45  0.2250 0.48 round=0.015
let steps_ped_die    = box -0.3375 0.3375 -0.3675   0.3375 1.3500 0.3675 round=0.015
let steps_ped_cap    = box -0.45   1.4850 -0.48    0.45  1.8000 0.48 round=0.015

let steps_base_x = mirror { cavetto 0.3375 0.2250 -0.60   0.45 0.3375 0.60 run=z } axis=x
let steps_base_z = mirror { cavetto -0.60 0.2250 0.3675   0.60 0.3375 0.48 run=x } axis=z
let steps_base_cx = box -0.3375 0.2250 -0.60    0.3375 0.3375 0.60
let steps_base_cz = box -0.60   0.2250 -0.3675  0.60   0.3375 0.3675
let steps_ped_base = intersection { union { steps_base_x steps_base_cx }
                                    union { steps_base_z steps_base_cz } }

let steps_bed_x = mirror { ovolo 0.3375 1.4850 -0.60   0.45 1.3500 0.60 run=z } axis=x
let steps_bed_z = mirror { ovolo -0.60 1.4850 0.3675   0.60 1.3500 0.48 run=x } axis=z
let steps_bed_cx = box -0.3375 1.3500 -0.60    0.3375 1.4850 0.60
let steps_bed_cz = box -0.60   1.3500 -0.3675  0.60   1.4850 0.3675
let steps_ped_bed = intersection { union { steps_bed_x steps_bed_cx }
                                   union { steps_bed_z steps_bed_cz } }

# The sunk panels. 0.045 (M/10) deep, inset 0.1125 (M/4) from the sides of the die, 0.54 (1.2 M)
# tall and sitting a little above its middle; on the south face and both flanks, and not on the
# north face, where the wall runs in. The cutters reach out past the face so the recess has a clean
# mouth after the manifest's 12 mm of grain.
#
# THESE ARE TABLETS AND NOT DOORS. The first pass ran them the full 0.7875 height of the die and,
# with porphyry in them, each pedestal read from thirty metres as a small building with a red front
# door. A panel is a panel when it is nearly square and has a margin you can see.
let steps_panel_s  = box -0.225  0.63 -0.50     0.225  1.17 -0.3225 round=0.01125
let steps_panel_e  = box  0.2925 0.63 -0.255    0.50   1.17  0.255  round=0.01125
let steps_panels   = union { steps_panel_s mirror { steps_panel_e } axis=x }

let steps_ped_head = union { steps_ped_bed steps_ped_cap }
let steps_ped_body = union { steps_ped_plinth steps_ped_base steps_ped_die }
let steps_ped_east = translate { difference { union { steps_ped_body steps_ped_head } steps_panels } }
                     9.00 0 -15.77

# --- folded ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Each piece folded on its own rather than as one lump, so that the paint below has a shape to key
# on for both sides of the building at once and never has to name a coordinate.

let steps_copings  = mirror { steps_coping_e } axis=x
let steps_walls    = mirror { steps_wall_e }   axis=x
let steps_pedestals = mirror { steps_ped_east } axis=x

let part_steps = union { steps_flight steps_copings steps_walls steps_pedestals }

# ================================================================================================
# PAINT
# ================================================================================================
#
# Granite for everything that is walked on, rained on and kicked; marble for the copings and the
# pedestal caps, which is the dressing a granite stair is given; porphyry in the sunk panels.
# Every rule keys on one of this file's own shapes, never on a coordinate.
#
# 0.02 FOR THE FLIGHT AND 0.035 FOR EVERYTHING ELSE, which is BRIEF.md rule 5 exactly. The 0.02 is
# for the manifest's 12 mm of grain, which pushes a voxel outside the shape its rule names. The
# 0.035 is for the other thing that does: a voxel is decided solid by COVERAGE and painted by a
# rule tested at the cell's CENTRE, and for a shape put where it is by `rotate` or `translate` the
# two do not agree. The flight is ten boxes drawn where they stand and takes 0.02; the cheek walls
# and the copings are a section turned about x and carried, and the pedestals, their caps and
# their inlays are all translated, so all five take 0.035.
#
# MEASURED, both arms, same geometry to the voxel (2,587,798), metre 32, on
# requests/steps-cuts-probe.clip — only the six thresholds differ:
#
#                       granite      marble    porphyry   base coat
#   all six at 0.02   2,515,774      67,788       4,212          24
#   five at 0.035     2,511,470      70,808       5,520           0
#
# The 24 is the visible half and the small half. The other 4,328 voxels did not fall through to the
# base coat at all — they were caught by the granite rule, which is written first and keyed on the
# whole cheek, so a voxel of a coping or an inlay that its own rule could not reach came out
# granite. A rind of pale limestone is obvious on a contact sheet; polished marble quietly coming
# out as rough granite along the edge of every coping is not.
#
# Later coats cover earlier ones, so this reads bottom upward: the whole cheek in granite first,
# then marble over the parts of it that are dressed, then the panels.

paint granite  where=steps_flight    below=0.02
paint granite  where=steps_walls     below=0.035
paint granite  where=steps_pedestals below=0.035

let steps_caps  = mirror { translate { steps_ped_head } 9.00 0 -15.77 } axis=x
paint marble   where=steps_copings   below=0.035
paint marble   where=steps_caps      below=0.035

# The panel key is not the cutter: it is pushed 0.045 further into the stone so that it reaches the
# floor of the recess, and pulled 0.0225 (M/20) in from its edges so that the 0.02 test does not
# spill a border of porphyry out onto the face of the die.
let steps_inlay_s = box -0.2025 0.6525 -0.50    0.2025 1.1475 -0.2775
let steps_inlay_e = box  0.2475 0.6525 -0.2325  0.50   1.1475  0.2325
let steps_inlays  = mirror { translate { union { steps_inlay_s mirror { steps_inlay_e } axis=x } }
                             9.00 0 -15.77 } axis=x
paint porphyry where=steps_inlays    below=0.035
